WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.060
Which players can you think of that have been the most fun for you to watch over the years?

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:12.540
Well, [Billy] Cannon, [Bert] Jones, [Johnny] Robinson, [Warren] Rabb.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:22.940
It’s not fair to name just a few because all of them . . . and I’ve become personal friends with a lot of them.

4
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:35.200
But all of the . . . all of the guys really have done . . . a lot of them have done . . . done a huge job in athletics here.

5
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.707
And there was a time, now, when some of these boys like . . . well,

6
00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:45.100
Gaynell Tinsley, for example, is a longtime friend from Claiborne Parish,

7
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:51.360
graduated at Homer [High School] up there and was . . . his first coaching job in high school was at Haynesville High School up there.

8
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.540
My father gave him his job, his first coaching job after he got hurt in the pros.

9
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:04.520
There’s no greater player in history, in my opinion, than Gaynell Tinsley.

10
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:10.320
He’s surely the greatest player LSU has ever produced and he still lives here in town.

11
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.340
You saw Charlie Alexander perhaps?

12
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.420
He works here in the office.

13
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:25.420
There really has been no greater running back in the history of the game here at LSU than Charles Alexander from Galveston, Texas.

14
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.420
So there are just a lot of them.

15
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.720
Billy Cannon, of course, lives here.

16
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.140
He’s a great, great player.

17
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:39.500
But I mean you could . . . We could sit here for two or three hours and name players and I could tell you something about each one of them.

18
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:47.060
But the overall picture that I get when I think about things like is that all of these fellows helped to make LSU,

19
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:57.860
really . . . In past years—though I’m quick to say it’s not that way right now [1993]—made LSU’s past years the scourge of the Deep South.

20
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:04.000
You know, other teams would come in here to play football and they’d be scared to death.

21
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.420
The players would really fear for their own lives when they come in here.

22
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.220
And for good reason, because we had great teams.
